1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:04,800
We're looking at these really amazing and powerful things called functions.

2
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:09,800
These are these sort of black boxes in a sense, that have all sorts of code inside of them.

3
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:12,600
We can call them and they can return values for us.

4
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:16,440
Now we know that there are lots and lots of built in functions, and there's all sorts of modules.

5
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:23,080
So you can have functions from everything from drawing type function to math functions, etc. and we

6
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:25,320
can use those functions to do lots of amazing things.

7
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:28,560
But the real power comes in when you start to create your own functions.

8
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:30,120
Why would you want to do this?

9
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:32,200
Well, it allows you to have this reusable code.

10
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:36,760
And as we'll show you now in this lesson, when you start to create functions, it makes it so much

11
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:42,200
easier to actually code because once the function is done once, it's like an amazing little magic box

12
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:44,400
that you can just use over and over and over again.

13
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:45,480
So let's have a look.

14
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:51,240
We're going to start off with a relatively simple example, just so I can show you the layout and the

15
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:54,280
syntax and basically what a function looks like.

16
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:58,240
So this is what you do to create your own function.

17
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:04,210
You start off by typing tapping this keyword def, which basically stands for define.

18
00:01:04,250 --> 00:01:08,770
Because every time you type in a new function, you're basically defining a new function.

19
00:01:08,930 --> 00:01:12,610
The next thing you want is to give your function a name.

20
00:01:13,090 --> 00:01:19,930
So in this example I'm just going to show you how to create a function that cubes a number.

21
00:01:20,170 --> 00:01:24,170
So obviously just like variables you're going to want to give your function a name that makes sense.

22
00:01:24,170 --> 00:01:27,610
So let me just call my function cube.

23
00:01:27,810 --> 00:01:33,930
And then functions also have the same thing with variables where you shouldn't start with a capital

24
00:01:33,930 --> 00:01:37,210
letter and you shouldn't start with a number.

25
00:01:37,930 --> 00:01:38,490
Okay.

26
00:01:38,970 --> 00:01:41,210
So there's define cube.

27
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:47,610
The next thing we need to make this cube function work we need a parameter in here which is going to

28
00:01:47,650 --> 00:01:51,570
hold the number that the user wants to cube.

29
00:01:51,690 --> 00:01:54,890
So in other words it's basically a variable called num.

30
00:01:55,690 --> 00:02:00,460
So this is the value that's going to be passed to the function so that it can cube that number.

31
00:02:00,540 --> 00:02:02,660
So this parameter will hold that value.

32
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:05,660
Just like when you chose to call print.

33
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:09,340
Well when we did examples with print you put in a value in those brackets.

34
00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:14,220
And that's how the function knew what sentence or what you wanted it to print.

35
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:15,060
Okay.

36
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:17,500
And then so how would we cube a number.

37
00:02:17,940 --> 00:02:25,100
Basically what we want to do is we want to take that number times it by itself again and times it again.

38
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:28,820
And that will give us the cube of the number.

39
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:36,380
But now with functions, you want this answer to be given back to the user or returned to the user,

40
00:02:36,380 --> 00:02:37,140
as we would say.

41
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:42,780
So what you actually want to do is you want to type in this word here called return.

42
00:02:43,580 --> 00:02:46,300
Now I'm going to show you what happens in this one that works.

43
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:48,660
And then I'm going to show you what happens if we take it away.

44
00:02:48,980 --> 00:02:55,380
So what this should do is the user is going to call the function by just typing the function's name.

45
00:02:55,540 --> 00:03:00,180
And then because we have this parameter Here we have one parameter called number.

46
00:03:00,420 --> 00:03:05,220
That means in order to call this function, the user has to put in a parameter.

47
00:03:06,660 --> 00:03:08,220
Let's just say seven.

48
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:12,980
If the user didn't put in a parameter yet, that means that this.

49
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:18,700
That means that Python will not see this cube function as being the same as this one.

50
00:03:19,620 --> 00:03:22,940
Let's just, for example, say that I'm put it in another variable here.

51
00:03:22,940 --> 00:03:24,620
Let's just call it Num2.

52
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:28,220
This now no longer calls this function over here.

53
00:03:28,220 --> 00:03:34,140
So you've got to check that your function has the same amount of parameters as the function you are

54
00:03:34,180 --> 00:03:35,700
wanting to call.

55
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:37,060
Okay.

56
00:03:37,620 --> 00:03:41,980
And then obviously we want to print this value.

57
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:45,820
If I didn't put the print statement well I'll show you what will happen.

58
00:03:45,860 --> 00:03:46,220
Okay.

59
00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:49,500
So all this is doing is it's taking this number seven.

60
00:03:50,180 --> 00:03:54,060
And this number seven is becoming this variable num.

61
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:59,830
Just like we assign variables NUM becomes seven and then it's just going seven times seven times seven,

62
00:03:59,830 --> 00:04:02,070
and it is returning that value to us.

63
00:04:02,750 --> 00:04:04,350
Interestingly what's actually happening here.

64
00:04:04,390 --> 00:04:06,790
We're running a function within a function.

65
00:04:06,790 --> 00:04:10,230
So the print function is obviously Python's own function.

66
00:04:10,550 --> 00:04:14,270
And we are passing that the result of our own function which was cube.

67
00:04:14,270 --> 00:04:18,670
So cube does something returns the result, hands it to the print function which does something which

68
00:04:18,670 --> 00:04:19,670
prints it on the screen.

69
00:04:19,670 --> 00:04:21,470
So you can see how powerful these little things are.

70
00:04:21,470 --> 00:04:23,750
Because one statement could do all of that.

71
00:04:24,230 --> 00:04:24,590
Yeah.

72
00:04:24,590 --> 00:04:29,910
So I also wanted to show you that this return value doesn't actually print anything.

73
00:04:30,070 --> 00:04:36,190
All it does is it returns this value to this function call.

74
00:04:36,230 --> 00:04:40,150
So if you never printed it you would actually it's almost a pointless function.

75
00:04:40,150 --> 00:04:41,830
But if I change this to print.

76
00:04:44,350 --> 00:04:47,590
Then it's going to print the value because it's going num becomes seven.

77
00:04:47,590 --> 00:04:49,950
And we're just doing like a normal print statement.

78
00:04:49,950 --> 00:04:52,470
Print seven times seven times seven.

79
00:04:52,510 --> 00:04:56,560
Now, the reason we wouldn't really want to do that is we want it to be a cube function, because you

80
00:04:56,560 --> 00:04:58,680
might now want to do a calculation with it.

81
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:03,800
And so rather than necessarily printing it the moment that Sarah's got cube seven so she could go cube

82
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:10,080
seven plus three and we could print that result or store that result in a variable, do whatever we

83
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:10,680
wanted.

84
00:05:10,840 --> 00:05:15,080
So now you can see how powerful it is, because we now have this function that can actually be used

85
00:05:15,080 --> 00:05:16,280
as part of a formula.

86
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:16,800
Okay.

87
00:05:16,840 --> 00:05:23,200
So there you can see what I did is I now could add three to the cube of seven because it's returned

88
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:24,200
an integer.

89
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:30,200
So because it's returned integer or returned a number I can add I can subtract and do all those other

90
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:31,840
fun operations.

91
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:35,720
So that's the basic layout of a function.

92
00:05:35,720 --> 00:05:43,520
Just remember you have this diff, you have your name and then you have these brackets with option with

93
00:05:43,520 --> 00:05:44,880
optional parameters.

94
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:46,960
Some functions don't actually need this.

95
00:05:46,960 --> 00:05:47,680
One does.

96
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:51,040
We'll get to examples where you may not need any parameters at all.

97
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:55,240
But for now know that is the layout of a function.

